---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Screenshot from '../components/Screenshot.astro';
import OsIcon from '../components/OsIcon.astro';
import Command from '../components/Command.astro';
import Limits from '../components/Limits.astro';
import { RELEASE, REPO_URL } from '../site';
---

<Base
  fullTitle="A private, open-source email client for Mac · OpenWren"
  description="OpenWren is a free, open-source email client for Mac. Gmail and IMAP in one inbox, mail encrypted on your Mac, and search across all accounts in milliseconds."
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <h1>A private, open-source email client for Mac</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      OpenWren is a free email client for macOS. All your accounts share one inbox and one search box, your mail is
      kept on your Mac in an encrypted database, and trackers are removed before you read a message. There is no
      subscription and no account to create.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href={RELEASE.mac}><OsIcon os="macos" /> Download {RELEASE.version} for macOS</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/download/#macos">Install steps</a>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">{RELEASE.macNote}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <Screenshot src="inbox-dark.png" alt="The OpenWren inbox in dark mode: folders, the message list and an open message" label="Inbox, dark" width={760} />

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>
      Apple Mail is fine until you have several accounts and years of mail and need to find one message. Most of the
      well-known alternatives on the Mac ask for a subscription, send your mail through their own servers to offer
      extras, or both. OpenWren is for people who want a plain, quick mail program that does neither.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Search across every account.</strong> One search box, results grouped by year, about 30 ms on 200,000 messages. It does not depend on Spotlight. <a href="/search/">How search works</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Nothing in between.</strong> OpenWren talks to your mail servers and to nobody else. There is no OpenWren server that sees your mail. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> lists every request.</li>
      <li><strong>Encrypted on disk.</strong> Mail and the search index are in one encrypted database. Its key and your passwords are in the macOS Keychain.</li>
      <li><strong>Trackers removed, images blocked</strong> until you ask for them, and warnings on mail that looks forged.</li>
      <li><strong>Open source.</strong> Every line is in the <a href={REPO_URL}>repository</a> under the Apache 2.0 licence.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Installing on macOS</h2>
    <p class="note">
      OpenWren is not signed with an Apple certificate yet, so macOS blocks the first start until you do one extra
      step. It has been tested on macOS 14 with an Intel processor, and not yet tried on Apple silicon.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the downloaded file and drag OpenWren into Applications.</li>
      <li>Open OpenWren. macOS refuses the first time, saying it cannot check the app or that it "is damaged". Click OK.</li>
      <li>Open Terminal, run this once, and open OpenWren again:</li>
    </ol>
    <Command code="xattr -cr /Applications/OpenWren.app" />
    <p>
      The command removes the mark macOS puts on downloaded files. The checksum of the download is in
      <a href={RELEASE.checksums}>SHA256SUMS</a> if you want to compare it first.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does not do</h2>
    <Limits />

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <h3>Does it work with iCloud Mail?</h3>
    <p>
      iCloud Mail offers IMAP with an app-specific password from your Apple account, which is the kind of account
      OpenWren supports. Enter the server names by hand if they are not guessed from your address.
    </p>
    <h3>Does it work with Gmail?</h3>
    <p>Yes, with an <a href="/docs/#gmail-with-an-app-password">app password</a>. Gmail labels are shown as folders.</p>
    <h3>Is there an iPhone app?</h3>
    <p>No. OpenWren is a desktop program for macOS, Windows and Linux.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
